936\",\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": \"10.1145/1993636.1993682\",\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"Ann. Symp. on Theory of Computing https://doi.org/ ACM\",\n          \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1145/1993636.1993682\",\n          \"year\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": \"10.4230/lipics\",\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"experiments. In 32nd Computational Complexity Conf. https://doi.org/\",\n          \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs\",\n          \"year\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": \"10.5061/dryad.k6t1rj8\",\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"public Dryad repository https://doi.org/ . 29. Ofek N. et al. Extending the lifetime of a quantum bit with error correction in\",\n          \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.5061/dryad.k6t1rj8\",\n          \"year\": null\n        }\n      ],\n      \"related_link_enrichment\": {\n        \"skip_reason\": null,\n        \"sources\": [\n          \"references\"\n        ],\n        \"status\": \"enriched\",\n        \"warnings\": [\n          \"Skipped 3 unsupported external-link candidate(s).\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"schema_version\": \"source_record_v1\",\n      \"source_date_inference\": {\n        \"confidence\": \"high\",\n        \"date\": \"2019-10-23\",\n        \"origin\": \"sources/artifacts/source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8/source.md\",\n        \"rationale\": \"The extracted markdown header states 'Published online: 23 October 2019'.\"\n      },\n      \"source_dossier\": {\n        \"sections\": [\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The source's main proposition is that Sycamore performed random circuit sampling in a classically inaccessible regime. The experiment uses 53 active qubits and deep random circuits to create a state space of about 10^16 possibilities, then asks the processor to sample bitstrings from the resulting distribution rather than solve a practical application directly.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"0\",\n              \"1\",\n              \"6\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Central Benchmark Claim\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"Cross-entropy benchmarking supplies the quantitative bridge between measured bitstrings and ideal circuit probabilities. In tractable regimes, ideal probabilities can be computed classically and compared with the quantum samples; in the largest regime, the same method becomes too costly, so verification has to be transferred through carefully simplified circuit families.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"2\",\n              \"3\",\n              \"7\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Verification Method\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The processor evidence is not just a headline qubit count. The article reports simultaneous-operation error rates for gates and readout, then uses those rates to predict total circuit fidelity. This matters because the supremacy claim would fail if large-scale simultaneous operation introduced uncontrolled correlated errors not visible in isolated gate calibration.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"4\",\n              \"5\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Hardware Error Evidence\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The hardest full circuits are not directly verified by classical simulation. Instead, the article compares full, patch, elided, and predicted fidelities where verification is possible, then uses elided circuits at 53 qubits and 20 cycles as the closest auditable proxy. The inference is therefore empirical and model-supported rather than a direct full-circuit probability check.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"3\",\n              \"7\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Supremacy-Regime Inference\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The dramatic runtime gap depends on matching the quantum task to an equal-fidelity classical sampling estimate. Sycamore's reported 200 seconds for one million samples is compared with 10,000 years on one million cores, but this is an extrapolated benchmark for random circuit sampling, not evidence that all valuable computations are now faster on quantum hardware.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"1\",\n              \"6\",\n              \"8\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Classical Cost Comparison\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The article explicitly separates this NISQ-era milestone from full-scale fault-tolerant quantum computing. It argues that quantum speedup is achievable in a real physical device, while acknowledging that applications such as Shor-style factoring still require logical qubits and major engineering advances beyond the reported noisy processor.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"1\",\n              \"8\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Limits and Interpretation\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"summary_long\": \"The article presents a landmark random-circuit sampling experiment using Google's Sycamore superconducting processor. Its central claim is that a programmable 53-qubit device sampled from a distribution generated by pseudo-random quantum circuits in a regime where equal-fidelity classical sampling was estimated to be prohibitively expensive. The source grounds this claim in several layers of evidence. First, it defines a benchmark whose classical difficulty grows with both qubit number and circuit depth because random circuits lack exploitable structure. Second, it uses linear cross-entropy benchmarking to compare measured bitstrings with ideal probabilities wherever classical simulation remains possible. Third, it measures component error rates for single-qubit gates, two-qubit gates, cycles, and readout under simultaneous operation, then uses those rates to predict whole-circuit fidelity. The decisive regime cannot be fully verified by direct classical simulation, so the article relies on patch, elided, and verification circuits to test whether lower-complexity variants track full-circuit behavior. For the largest elided 53-qubit, 20-cycle data set, the reported F_XEB statistic supports fidelity above 0.1% at 5\\u03c3 confidence. Against this measured fidelity, the authors compare Sycamore's 200-second runtime for one million samples with an extrapolated 10,000-year classical sampling cost on one million cores. The practical interpretation is deliberately narrow: the result demonstrates a super-classical benchmark computation in a noisy intermediate-scale device, not immediate fault-tolerant quantum computing or a broad application such as factoring. Its importance lies in showing that controlled, many-qubit quantum dynamics can be engineered, benchmarked, and made classically inaccessible for a specific task.\",\n        \"summary_short\": \"This Nature article reports Sycamore random-circuit sampling on 53 qubits. Cross-entropy benchmarking, component error calibration, and classical-cost estimates support a task-specific quantum supremacy claim. The result is a benchmark speedup, not general fault-tolerant quantum computing.\"\n      },\n      \"source_extraction\": {\n        \"converter_name\": \"pdftotext\",\n        \"converter_version\": null,\n        \"quality_status\": \"usable\",\n        \"status\": \"success\",\n        \"warnings\": []\n      },\n      \"source_id\": \"source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8\",\n      \"source_kind\": \"file\",\n      \"source_locator\": \"02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor.pdf\",\n      \"source_media_type\": \"application/pdf\",\n      \"source_semantic\": {\n        \"authors\": [\n          \"Frank Arute\",\n          \"Kunal Arya\",\n          \"Ryan Babbush\",\n          \"Dave Bacon\",\n          \"Joseph C. Bardin\",\n          \"Rami Barends\",\n          \"Rupak Biswas\",\n          \"Sergio Boixo\",\n          \"Fernando G. S. L. Brandao\",\n          \"David A. Buell\",\n          \"Brian Burkett\",\n          \"Yu Chen\",\n          \"Zijun Chen\",\n          \"Ben Chiaro\",\n          \"Roberto Collins\",\n          \"William Courtney\",\n          \"Andrew Dunsworth\",\n          \"Edward Farhi\",\n          \"Brooks Foxen\",\n          \"Austin Fowler\",\n          \"Craig Gidney\",\n          \"Marissa Giustina\",\n          \"Rob Graff\",\n          \"Keith Guerin\",\n          \"Steve Habegger\",\n          \"Matthew P. Harrigan\",\n          \"Michael J. Hartmann\",\n          \"Alan Ho\",\n          \"Markus Hoffmann\",\n          \"Trent Huang\",\n          \"Travis S. Humble\",\n          \"Sergei V. Isakov\",\n          \"Evan Jeffrey\",\n          \"Zhang Jiang\",\n          \"Dvir Kafri\",\n          \"Kostyantyn Kechedzhi\",\n          \"Julian Kelly\",\n          \"Paul V. Klimov\",\n          \"Sergey Knysh\",\n          \"Alexander Korotkov\",\n          \"Fedor Kostritsa\",\n          \"David Landhuis\",\n          \"Mike Lindmark\",\n          \"Erik Lucero\",\n          \"Dmitry Lyakh\",\n          \"Salvatore Mandr\\u00e0\",\n          \"Jarrod R. McClean\",\n          \"Matthew McEwen\",\n          \"Anthony Megrant\",\n          \"Xiao Mi\",\n          \"Kristel Michielsen\",\n          \"Masoud Mohseni\",\n          \"Josh Mutus\",\n          \"Ofer Naaman\",\n          \"Matthew Neeley\",\n          \"Charles Neill\",\n          \"Murphy Yuezhen Niu\",\n          \"Eric Ostby\",\n          \"Andre Petukhov\",\n          \"John C. Platt\",\n          \"Chris Quintana\",\n          \"Eleanor G. Rieffel\",\n          \"Pedram Roushan\",\n          \"Nicholas C. Rubin\",\n          \"Daniel Sank\",\n          \"Kevin J. Satzinger\",\n          \"Vadim Smelyanskiy\",\n          \"Kevin J. Sung\",\n          \"Matthew D. Trevithick\",\n          \"Amit Vainsencher\",\n          \"Benjamin Villalonga\",\n          \"Theodore White\",\n          \"Z. Jamie Yao\",\n          \"Ping Yeh\",\n          \"Adam Zalcman\",\n          \"Hartmut Neven\",\n          \"John M. Martinis\"\n        ],\n        \"display_title\": \"Quantum Supremacy Using a Programmable Superconducting Processor\",\n        \"doi\": \"10.1038/s41586-019-1666-5\",\n        \"source_id\": \"source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8\"\n      },\n      \"source_type\": \"file\",\n      \"summary\": \"The source reports Google's Sycamore experiment demonstrating task-specific quantum supremacy in random circuit sampling. It combines a 53-qubit programmable superconducting processor, cross-entropy benchmarking, component error modeling, and classical simulation cost estimates to argue that Sycamore performed a sampling task in minutes that would take state-of-the-art classical resources thousands of years at equal fidelity.\",\n      \"title\": \"02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor\",\n      \"warnings\": [\n        {\n          \"code\": \"date_inferred_from_markdown\",\n          \"message\": \"Task context supplied date=null, but source.md contains 'Published online: 23 October 2019', so source_date_inference was populated from the markdown.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"code\": \"noisy_reference_extraction\",\n          \"message\": \"The source_record reference list contains malformed or noisy extracted entries; claims and evidence were grounded in source_extraction.json and source.md instead.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"code\": \"full_supremacy_data_not_directly_verified\",\n          \"message\": \"The hardest full-circuit samples were archived but not directly classically simulated in the source; fidelity support comes from elided and verification-circuit evidence.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"analysis_policy\": {\n        \"fallback_artifacts\": [\n          \"source_file\"\n        ],\n        \"preferred_artifact\": \"source_markdown\",\n        \"quality_status\": \"usable\",\n        \"warnings\": []\n      },\n      \"article_kind\": null,\n      \"artifact_root\": \"sources/artifacts/source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\",\n      \"artifacts\": {\n        \"front_page_image\": \"sources/artifacts/source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2/front_page.png\",\n        \"source_extraction\": \"sources/artifacts/source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2/source_extraction.json\",\n        \"source_file\": \"sources/artifacts/source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2/source.pdf\",\n        \"source_markdown\": \"sources/artifacts/source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2/source.md\",\n        \"source_provenance\": \"sources/artifacts/source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2/source_provenance.md\"\n      },\n      \"authors\": [\n        \"H. J. Kimble\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_count\": null,\n      \"citation_count_as_of\": null,\n      \"citation_count_confidence\": \"unknown\",\n      \"citation_count_provider\": \"unknown\",\n      \"date\": null,\n      \"display_title\": \"Quantum Interconnects for the Quantum Internet\",\n      \"external_related_links\": [\n        {\n          \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n          \"domain\": \"doi.org\",\n          \"link_type\": \"canonical_paper\",\n          \"provenance\": {\n            \"origin\": \"reference_doi\",\n            \"source_field\": \"references[5].doi\"\n          },\n          \"quality_status\": \"trusted\",\n          \"rationale\": \"Derived from a DOI found in the source references.\",\n          \"title\": \"3 765-769 advance online publication 23 September DOI\",\n          \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1038/nphys726\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n          \"domain\": \"doi.org\",\n          \"link_type\": \"canonical_paper\",\n          \"provenance\": {\n            \"origin\": \"reference_doi\",\n            \"source_field\": \"references[6].doi\"\n          },\n          \"quality_status\": \"trusted\",\n          \"rationale\": \"Derived from a DOI found in the source references.\",\n          \"title\": \"April DOI\",\n          \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1140300\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n          \"domain\": \"arxiv.org\",\n          \"link_type\": \"research_index\",\n          \"provenance\": {\n            \"origin\": \"reference_url\",\n            \"source_field\": \"references[3].url\"\n          },\n          \"quality_status\": \"trusted\",\n          \"rationale\": \"Captured from normalized source references.\",\n          \"title\": \"http://arxiv.org/abs/\",\n          \"url\": \"http://arxiv.org/abs/0802.1475\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n          \"domain\": \"arxiv.org\",\n          \"link_type\": \"research_index\",\n          \"provenance\": {\n            \"origin\": \"reference_arxiv\",\n            \"source_field\": \"references[3].arxiv\"\n          },\n          \"quality_status\": \"trusted\",\n          \"rationale\": \"Derived from an arXiv identifier found in the source references.\",\n          \"title\": \"http://arxiv.org/abs/\",\n          \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/0802.1475\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n          \"domain\": \"arxiv.org\",\n          \"link_type\": \"research_index\",\n          \"provenance\": {\n            \"origin\": \"reference_arxiv\",\n            \"source_field\": \"references[4].arxiv\"\n          },\n          \"quality_status\": \"trusted\",\n          \"rationale\": \"Derived from an arXiv identifier found in the source references.\",\n          \"title\": \"quant-ph 25 Jun\",\n          \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.4195v1\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"ingested_at\": \"2026-04-26T16:04:50Z\",\n      \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n      \"references\": [\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": null,\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"investigated as surveyed in Ref. 2 and described at the following websites: map.shtml\",\n          \"url\": \"http://qist.lanl.gov/qcomp\",\n          \"year\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": null,\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"http://www.scala-ip.org/public/ and /. However my purpose is not to present an\",\n          \"url\": \"http://www.qubitapplications.com\",\n          \"year\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": null,\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"/ and http://www.qubitapplications.com/. However my purpose is not to present an\",\n          \"url\": \"http://www.scala-ip.org/public\",\n          \"year\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": \"0802.1475\",\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": null,\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"http://arxiv.org/abs/\",\n          \"url\": \"http://arxiv.org/abs/0802.1475\",\n          \"year\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": \"0806.4195v1\",\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": null,\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"quant-ph 25 Jun\",\n          \"url\": null,\n          \"year\": 2008\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": \"10.1038/nphys726\",\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"3 765-769 advance online publication 23 September DOI\",\n          \"url\": null,\n          \"year\": 2007\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": \"10.1126/science.1140300\",\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"April DOI\",\n          \"url\": null,\n          \"year\": 2007\n        }\n      ],\n      \"related_link_enrichment\": {\n        \"skip_reason\": null,\n        \"sources\": [\n          \"references\"\n        ],\n        \"status\": \"enriched\",\n        \"warnings\": [\n          \"Skipped 3 unsupported external-link candidate(s).\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"schema_version\": \"source_record_v1\",\n      \"source_date_inference\": {\n        \"confidence\": \"unknown\",\n        \"date\": null,\n        \"origin\": \"unknown\",\n        \"rationale\": \"The source record did not resolve a publication date. The markdown includes an arXiv stamp of 25 Jun 2008 and a separate generated-looking Dated line, so no normalized publication date is asserted.\"\n      },\n      \"source_dossier\": {\n        \"sections\": [\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The source's first move is architectural: quantum nodes linked only by classical channels do not realize the same state space as nodes joined by quantum channels. This matters because networked quantum processors cannot simply exchange measurement records and retain the same computational or metrological possibilities; they need channels that carry quantum states or distribute entanglement with high fidelity.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"0\",\n              \"1\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Network Motivation\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The proposed network depends on reversible conversion between stationary matter memories and flying photons. Kimble makes the requirement concrete through controllable coupling \\u03c7(t), useful bandwidth \\u03ba, parasitic loss \\u03b3, robustness to imperfections, and error handling. The interface is therefore both a physics problem and a systems problem: memory timing, transfer fidelity, and loss budgets all matter.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"0\",\n              \"2\",\n              \"3\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Light-Matter Interface Requirements\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"Cavity QED supplies one candidate hardware path by enhancing the electric field of a single photon in a small, high-Q resonator. In the strong-coupling regime, g dominates \\u03b3 and \\u03ba, and the critical photon and atom numbers fall below unity. Dark-state protocols then use shaped control pulses to absorb and re-emit photonic states while avoiding lossy excited-state population in the ideal limit.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"2\",\n              \"3\",\n              \"4\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Cavity-QED Route\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The ensemble approach replaces single trapped atoms with collective excitations shared over many atoms. DLCZ uses weak write pulses and indistinguishable single-photon detection to herald a remote entangled spin excitation, while EIT provides a deterministic memory mechanism for mapping optical states into and out of ensembles. These routes trade deterministic local control for collective enhancement and heralded operation.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"5\",\n              \"7\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Atomic-Ensemble Route\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The source grounds its optimism in reported milestones: efficient readout from stored collective excitations, heralded entanglement between ensembles separated by 3 m, inferred concurrence of 0.9 \\u00b1 0.3, Bell-inequality violation after memory readout, and early entanglement swapping. These are not full quantum repeaters, but they demonstrate several primitives needed for scalable nodes.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"6\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Experimental Evidence\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"Kimble emphasizes that making larger networks is not just a matter of adding more working links. Verification itself becomes hard because full density-matrix reconstruction grows exponentially with network size, while purely functional algorithm tests may lack diagnostic value when networks fail. This motivates physical, scalable, and task-relevant tests of multipartite entanglement.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"8\",\n              \"9\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Scaling Limits\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The paper's practical stance is ambitious but not triumphalist. It expects heterogeneous networks that combine cavity nodes, ensemble memories, integrated resonators, repeaters, and error-corrected teleportation. The near-term value is in assembling and validating primitives; the long-term value is a platform for quantum communication, distributed processing, metrology, and controlled many-body quantum dynamics.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"0\",\n              \"2\",\n              \"5\",\n              \"9\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Practical Interpretation\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"summary_long\": \"Kimble's source treats the quantum internet not as a near-term communication product but as a research program for connecting quantum systems without destroying their coherence. The central architectural idea is that quantum information must be generated, processed, and stored locally in nodes, then moved or entangled across channels by interfaces that reversibly translate states between matter and light. This distinguishes a fully quantum network from merely classically coordinated quantum processors: when channels preserve quantum coherence, the accessible state space scales across all nodes rather than remaining a sum of separate node spaces. The physical burden then falls on light-matter interfaces. In cavity QED, single atoms inside high-finesse resonators can reach strong coupling, allowing a single photon and a single atom to interact coherently; dark-state adiabatic protocols show how a photonic qubit can be absorbed into an atomic memory and later re-emitted. The source pairs that route with atomic-ensemble schemes, especially DLCZ, where weak write pulses, single-photon interference, and heralding create a shared collective spin excitation between distant ensembles. It cites experimental progress in retrieval efficiency, stored entanglement over meter-scale separation, Bell-inequality tests, entanglement swapping, and EIT-based reversible memory mapping. At the same time, the paper's practical message is restrained. The demonstrated pieces are primitive relative to full repeater-based, error-corrected networks, and complex networks create a verification problem because full density-matrix reconstruction scales exponentially. The mature quantum internet implied here is therefore heterogeneous: it will likely combine atom-cavity systems, ensemble memories, integrated resonators, quantum repeaters, local processing, and new entanglement diagnostics rather than rely on a single uniform device platform.\",\n        \"summary_short\": \"Kimble frames the quantum internet as nodes and channels built on reversible light-matter interfaces. It contrasts genuine quantum connectivity with classically linked quantum devices, then reviews cavity-QED and atomic-ensemble routes while emphasizing unresolved needs in memories, repeaters, teleportation, and scalable verification.\"\n      },\n      \"source_extraction\": {\n        \"converter_name\": \"pdftotext\",\n        \"converter_version\": null,\n        \"quality_status\": \"usable\",\n        \"status\": \"success\",\n        \"warnings\": []\n      },\n      \"source_id\": \"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\",\n      \"source_kind\": \"file\",\n      \"source_locator\": \"03-the-quantum-internet.pdf\",\n      \"source_media_type\": \"application/pdf\",\n      \"source_semantic\": {\n        \"authors\": [\n          \"H. J. Kimble\"\n        ],\n        \"display_title\": \"Quantum Interconnects for the Quantum Internet\",\n        \"original_title\": \"The Quantum Internet\",\n        \"source_id\": \"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\",\n        \"source_locator\": \"03-the-quantum-internet.pdf\"\n      },\n      \"source_type\": \"file\",\n      \"summary\": \"H. J. Kimble's The Quantum Internet frames quantum networks as systems of nodes and channels that require reversible light-matter interfaces, strong single-photon nonlinearities, ensemble memories, repeaters, and scalable verification. The source surveys cavity-QED and atomic-ensemble routes, emphasizing both experimental progress and the difficulty of extending and certifying entanglement in larger heterogeneous networks.\",\n      \"title\": \"03-the-quantum-internet\",\n      \"warnings\": [\n        {\n          \"code\": \"missing_publication_date\",\n          \"message\": \"Publication date could not be resolved from the source record; date remains null.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"code\": \"date_signal_conflict\",\n          \"message\": \"Markdown contains an arXiv stamp of 25 Jun 2008 and a separate Dated line reading November 26, 2024, so the output does not infer a normalized source date.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"code\": \"review_like_source\",\n          \"message\": \"Several evidence items summarize formal arguments or cited experimental results from a review-style source rather than new measurements introduced solely by this document.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"analysis_policy\": {\n        \"fallback_artifacts\": [\n          \"source_file\"\n        ],\n        \"preferred_artifact\": \"source_markdown\",\n        \"quality_status\": \"usable\",\n        \"warnings\": []\n      },\n      \"article_kind\": null,\n      \"artifact_root\": \"sources/artifacts/source-04-a-variational-eigenvalue-solver-on-a-photonic-quantum-processor--4678297edf5b\",\n      \"artifacts\": {\n        \"front_page_image\": \"sources/artifacts/source-04-a-variational-eigenvalue-solver-on-a-photonic-quantum-processor--4678297edf5b/front_page.png\",\n        \"source_extraction\": \"sources/artifacts/source-04-a-variational-eigenvalue-solver-on-a-photonic-quantum-processor--4678297edf5b/source_extraction.json\",\n        \"source_file\": \"sources/artifacts/source-04-a-variational-eigenvalue-solver-on-a-photonic-quantum-processor--4678297edf5b/source.pdf\",\n        \"source_markdown\": \"sources/artifacts/source-04-a-variational-eigenvalue-solver-on-a-photonic-quantum-processor--4678297edf5b/source.md\",\n        \"source_provenance\": \"sources/artifacts/source-04-a-variational-eigenvalue-solver-on-a-photonic-quantum-processor--4678297edf5b/source_provenance.md\"\n      },\n      \"authors\": [\n        \"Alberto Peruzzo\",\n        \"Jarrod McClean\",\n        \"Peter Shadbolt\",\n        \"Man-Hong Yung\",\n        \"Xiao-Qi Zhou\",\n        \"Peter J. Love\",\n        \"Al\\u00e1n Aspuru-Guzik\",\n        \"Jeremy L. O\\u2019Brien\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_count\": null,\n      \"citation_count_as_of\": null,\n      \"citation_count_confidence\": \"unknown\",\n      \"citation_count_provider\": \"unknown\",\n      \"date\": null,\n      \"display_title\": \"Photonic Variational Solver for Molecular Energies\",\n      \"external_related_links\": [\n        {\n          \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n          \"domain\": \"doi.org\",\n          \"link_type\": \"canonical_paper\",\n          \"provenance\": {\n            \"origin\": \"reference_doi\",\n            \"source_field\": \"references[3].doi\"\n          },\n          \"quality_status\": \"trusted\",\n          \"rationale\": \"Derived from a DOI found in the source references.\",\n          \"title\": \"Received 9 Dec | Accepted 27 May 2014 | Published 23 Jul 2014 OPEN\",\n          \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms5213\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n          \"domain\": \"arxiv.org\",\n          \"link_type\": \"research_index\",\n          \"provenance\": {\n            \"origin\": \"reference_url\",\n            \"source_field\": \"references[2].url\"\n          },\n          \"quality_status\": \"trusted\",\n          \"rationale\": \"Captured from normalized source references.\",\n          \"title\": \"optimization. Preprint at http://arxiv.org/abs/ . Joe Fitzsimons and James Whit\\ufb01eld for discussions. We acknowledge \\ufb01nancial support\",\n          \"url\": \"http://arxiv.org/abs/0804.4457\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n          \"domain\": \"arxiv.org\",\n          \"link_type\": \"research_index\",\n          \"provenance\": {\n            \"origin\": \"reference_arxiv\",\n            \"source_field\": \"references[2].arxiv\"\n          },\n          \"quality_status\": \"trusted\",\n          \"rationale\": \"Derived from an arXiv identifier found in the source references.\",\n          \"title\": \"optimization. Preprint at http://arxiv.org/abs/ . Joe Fitzsimons and James Whit\\ufb01eld for discussions. We acknowledge \\ufb01nancial support\",\n          \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/0804.4457\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"ingested_at\": \"2026-04-26T16:17:50Z\",\n      \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n      \"references\": [\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": null,\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"simulation of the wavefunction to probe frustrated Heisenberg spin systems. Reprints and permission information is available online at /\",\n          \"url\": \"http://npg.nature.com\",\n          \"year\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": null,\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"quantum computers. New J. Phys. 14 115023 . Supplementary Information accompanies this paper at /\",\n          \"url\": \"http://www.nature.com\",\n          \"year\": 2012\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": \"0804.4457\",\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": null,\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"optimization. Preprint at http://arxiv.org/abs/ . Joe Fitzsimons and James Whit\\ufb01eld for discussions. We acknowledge \\ufb01nancial support\",\n          \"url\": \"http://arxiv.org/abs/0804.4457\",\n          \"year\": 2008\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": \"10.1038/ncomms5213\",\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"Received 9 Dec | Accepted 27 May 2014 | Published 23 Jul 2014 OPEN\",\n          \"url\": null,\n          \"year\": 2013\n        }\n      ],\n      \"related_link_enrichment\": {\n        \"skip_reason\": null,\n        \"sources\": [\n          \"references\"\n        ],\n        \"status\": \"enriched\",\n        \"warnings\": [\n          \"Skipped 2 unsupported external-link candidate(s).\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"schema_version\": \"source_record_v1\",\n      \"source_date_inference\": {\n        \"confidence\": \"high\",\n        \"date\": \"2014-07-23\",\n        \"origin\": \"source_markdown_header\",\n        \"rationale\": \"The extracted source header states: Received 9 Dec 2013, Accepted 27 May 2014, Published 23 Jul 2014.\"\n      },\n      \"source_dossier\": {\n        \"sections\": [\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The central algorithmic move is to estimate <H> through a Pauli decomposition rather than through a long coherent phase-estimation circuit. For Hamiltonians with polynomially many terms, the QPU measures term expectations and the CPU adds weighted results. This establishes the measurement primitive behind the variational solver, while retaining a precision cost in repeated measurements.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"0\",\n              \"1\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Core Algorithm\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"QVE wraps QEE in a Rayleigh-Ritz minimization loop. The quantum device prepares a parameterized trial state and estimates the Hamiltonian expectation; the classical optimizer then updates the state parameters. The endpoint is not only an energy estimate but also a parameter prescription for reconstructing the approximate eigenstate.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"2\",\n              \"8\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Variational Feedback Loop\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The paper connects the small demonstration to chemistry by discussing unitary coupled cluster and device-defined ansatz families. The UCC argument matters because fixed excitation level gives polynomially many terms after transformation, while the device-ansatz view admits circuits tailored to available hardware and coherence depth.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"3\",\n              \"9\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Ansatz Significance\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The experiment uses an integrated photonic processor rather than an abstract circuit simulation. Photon pairs encode the two-qubit input, phase shifters set state and measurement parameters, a CNOT supplies entangling capability, and detector counts feed the CPU. This grounds the feasibility claim in real optical hardware with identifiable strengths and imperfections.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"4\",\n              \"7\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Photonic Implementation\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The main experimental target is the He-H+ dissociation curve in a minimal basis. The reported corrected minimum at 92.3\\u00b10.1 pm and -2.865\\u00b10.008 MJ mol^-1 shows that the feedback loop can locate a chemically meaningful ground-state curve for a four-dimensional Hamiltonian, though not yet at industrial chemical scale.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"5\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Molecular Demonstration\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The method saves coherent gate depth by replacing a serial QPE process with short coherent measurements and repeated preparations. The paper\\u2019s 4x4 comparison, one CNOT versus at least twelve for QPE, illustrates the benefit. The non-free part of the trade is extra repetitions and sensitivity to variation across preparations.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"1\",\n              \"6\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Resource Tradeoff\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The authors explicitly identify sources of error: photon distinguishability, higher-order photon components, imperfect beamsplitters and phase calibration, coupling imbalance, and increased optimization difficulty at larger separations. These details keep the result from being overread as scalable chemistry, but they also identify clear hardware-improvement paths.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"7\",\n              \"8\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Experimental Limits\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"summary_long\": \"This Nature Communications article is a proof-of-principle demonstration of a hybrid variational eigenvalue solver, now recognizable as an early VQE-style architecture. The paper begins from the bottleneck that quantum phase estimation can estimate eigenvalues efficiently but demands long coherent controlled evolutions, which were far beyond near-term photonic hardware. Its alternative decomposes Hamiltonian expectation values into sums of Pauli-product measurements, estimates those terms on a quantum processing unit, and lets a classical optimizer choose the next state-preparation parameters. The quantum processor therefore supplies expectation values and state preparation, while the CPU performs aggregation and feedback. The experimental platform is a reconfigurable integrated photonic chip using downconversion photon pairs, voltage-controlled phase shifters, one CNOT gate, and photon-counting measurements. With this apparatus, the authors compute the ground state of a 4x4 full-configuration-interaction Hamiltonian for He-H+ at multiple bond lengths. After correcting systematic errors, they report an equilibrium bond length of 92.3\\u00b10.1 pm and ground-state electronic energy of -2.865\\u00b10.008 MJ mol^-1, with more than 96% of data points within chemical accuracy relative to the theoretical curve. The source is careful about limitations: the result depends on repeated state preparations, finite photon count rates, systematic photonic imperfections, and a small molecular problem. Its broader significance is architectural rather than chemical scale. It shows that useful eigenvalue experiments can be organized around shallow quantum circuits and classical optimization, opening a route for near-term processors that cannot sustain full phase-estimation circuits.\",\n        \"summary_short\": \"Peruzzo and collaborators present an early experimental variational quantum eigensolver implemented with integrated photonics. The work replaces phase-estimation-style long coherent evolution with repeated short Pauli measurements plus classical feedback, then demonstrates the method on the minimal-basis He-H+ ground-state energy curve.\"\n      },\n      \"source_extraction\": {\n        \"converter_name\": \"pdftotext\",\n        \"converter_version\": null,\n        \"quality_status\": \"usable\",\n        \"status\": \"success\",\n        \"warnings\": []\n      },\n      \"source_id\": \"source-04-a-variational-eigenvalue-solver-on-a-photonic-quantum-processor--4678297edf5b\",\n      \"source_kind\": \"file\",\n      \"source_locator\": \"04-a-variational-eigenvalue-solver-on-a-photonic-quantum-processor.pdf\",\n      \"source_media_type\": \"application/pdf\",\n      \"source_semantic\": {\n        \"authors\": [\n          \"Alberto Peruzzo\",\n          \"Jarrod McClean\",\n          \"Peter Shadbolt\",\n          \"Man-Hong Yung\",\n          \"Xiao-Qi Zhou\",\n          \"Peter J. Love\",\n          \"Al\\u00e1n Aspuru-Guzik\",\n          \"Jeremy L. O\\u2019Brien\"\n        ],\n        \"display_title\": \"Photonic Variational Solver for Molecular Energies\",\n        \"doi\": \"10.1038/ncomms5213\",\n        \"journal\": \"Nature Communications\",\n        \"published_date\": \"2014-07-23\",\n        \"source_id\": \"source-04-a-variational-eigenvalue-solver-on-a-photonic-quantum-processor--4678297edf5b\"\n      },\n      \"source_type\": \"file\",\n      \"summary\": \"The source introduces and experimentally demonstrates a hybrid quantum-classical variational eigenvalue solver on an integrated photonic processor. Its central result is a proof-of-principle He-H+ molecular-energy calculation that trades QPE-style long coherent evolution for repeated short expectation-value measurements and classical optimization.\",\n      \"title\": \"04-a-variational-eigenvalue-solver-on-a-photonic-quantum-processor\",\n      \"warnings\": [\n        {\n          \"code\": \"source_record_placeholder_title\",\n          \"message\": \"The source record display title was 'Untitled Source'; the paper title, authors, DOI, and publication date were recovered from the extracted source markdown.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"code\