      \"doi\": \"10.1103/physrevx.4.041012\",\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"041012 . DOI . Defines closeness mea\",\n          \"url\": null,\n          \"year\": 2014\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": \"1401.2910\",\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": \"10.1126/science.1252319\",\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"345 420\\u2013424 . DOI\",\n          \"url\": null,\n          \"year\": 2014\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": \"5278.1073\",\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": \"10.1126/science.273.5278.1073\",\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"DOI\",\n          \"url\": null,\n          \"year\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": \"10.1126/science.aag2302\",\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"/\",\n          \"url\": \"http://science.sciencemag.org/content\",\n          \"year\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": \"10.1137/s0097539795293172\",\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"DOI\",\n          \"url\": null,\n          \"year\": 1997\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": \"10.1140/epjqt/s40507-015-\",\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"EPJ Quantum Technology 2 17 . DOI\",\n          \"url\": null,\n          \"year\": 2014\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": \"10.1140/epjqt/s40507-015-0023-\",\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"EPJ Quantum Technol. 2 10 . DOI\",\n          \"url\": null,\n          \"year\": 2015\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": \"10.1140/epjst/e2015-02349-9\",\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": null,\n          \"url\": null,\n          \"year\": null\n        },\n        {\n          \"arxiv\": null,\n          \"authors\": [],\n          \"doi\": \"10.1209/0295-5075/99/57004\",\n          \"linked_source_ids\": [],\n          \"title\": \"Europhys. Lett. 99 57004 . DOI\",\n          \"url\": null,\n          \"year\": 2012\n        }\n      ],\n      \"related_link_enrichment\": {\n        \"skip_reason\": null,\n        \"sources\": [\n          \"references\"\n        ],\n        \"status\": \"enriched\",\n        \"warnings\": [\n          \"Skipped 3 unsupported external-link candidate(s).\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"schema_version\": \"source_record_v1\",\n      \"source_date_inference\": {\n        \"confidence\": \"unknown\",\n        \"date\": null,\n        \"origin\": \"task_context\",\n        \"rationale\": \"Publication date could not be resolved by the ingest context; the markdown shows a manuscript date of May 14, 2018 but this is not treated as a resolved publication date.\"\n      },\n      \"source_dossier\": {\n        \"sections\": [\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The review's core argument is that quantum machine learning is plausible because quantum processors can manipulate patterns and amplitudes in ways classical machines may not efficiently simulate. That claim is not treated as a blanket guarantee; the source anchors it in specific mechanisms such as query complexity, gate complexity, qBLAS, and sampling. The field is therefore presented as a search for tasks where quantum structure survives the full learning pipeline.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"0\",\n              \"1\",\n              \"9\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Core Argument\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The most developed path runs through quantum linear algebra. qPCA, HHL, support vector machines, regression, and optimization inherit their promise from subroutines for Fourier transforms, eigenvalue estimation, eigenvector extraction, and linear-system solving. These are strong mathematical hooks for speedup, but the review emphasizes that they usually return quantum states or summary statistics rather than complete classical data products.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"1\",\n              \"2\",\n              \"3\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Linear Algebra Pathway\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The source repeatedly warns that loading classical data and reading classical answers can dominate the cost of a quantum algorithm. qRAM can make some encodings efficient in principle, but large coherent memory arrays are technologically difficult, and extracting full vectors or principal components can require exponentially many measurements. This turns many headline speedups into conditional, pipeline-level hypotheses.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"4\",\n              \"5\",\n              \"9\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Input And Output Limits\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"Deep quantum learning is presented as a hardware-conscious route that may not need a large universal quantum computer. Quantum annealers, photonic circuits, and quantum Boltzmann machines can instantiate learning models directly as physical systems. The evidence is promising but scoped: thousand-spin annealer demonstrations and quadratic sampling or thermalization arguments show feasibility signals, not universal superiority.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"6\",\n              \"7\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Deep Quantum Learning\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"Quantum data is the cleanest setting for the review's logic because the data need not first be converted from classical records into amplitudes. For density matrices, dynamics, and measurement streams from quantum devices, algorithms can seek principal components or expectation-value summaries directly. This can reduce tomography burdens and supports applications in device characterization and quantum-state learning.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"8\",\n              \"9\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Quantum Data Advantage\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"body\": \"The practical takeaway is cautious optimism. The review identifies credible mechanisms for quantum advantage and credible hardware trajectories, including annealers, photonic chips, cloud-accessible processors, and qRAM research. But it treats input, output, costing, and benchmarking as first-class scientific problems, meaning a useful quantum machine learning result must demonstrate complete workflow advantage, not just an isolated fast subroutine.\",\n            \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\n              \"0\",\n              \"4\",\n              \"5\",\n              \"9\"\n            ],\n            \"heading\": \"Practical Interpretation\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"summary_long\": \"The source is a broad review of quantum machine learning, using the field's strongest algorithmic ideas while repeatedly distinguishing asymptotic promise from implementable advantage. It begins from the observation that quantum systems can generate patterns that classical systems may not efficiently reproduce; if quantum processors can generate such patterns, quantum learning systems may also recognize or model them. The review then formalizes speedup in terms of query and gate complexity, because practical superiority over classical machine learning is hard to prove and must eventually be benchmarked against optimized heuristics. Its most important technical pathway is quantum linear algebra: qBLAS primitives, HHL-style linear-system solving, qPCA, support vector machines, and related optimization routines can in principle give exponential or polynomial improvements for suitable problem encodings. The source is careful, however, to state that these gains depend on state preparation, qRAM, sparsity, conditioning, and output requirements. A quantum state proportional to a solution vector is not the same as a cheaply available classical answer. The review also treats deep quantum learning as a separate route, where special-purpose hardware such as quantum annealers, photonic circuits, and quantum Boltzmann machines may be relevant before large universal machines exist. In that setting, speedups arise from thermalization, sampling, and training-data access, but they still rely on hardware and access assumptions. The most natural domain may be quantum data itself: when data are already quantum states, quantum routines can target principal components, dynamics, or expectation values without reconstructing the whole state. Overall, the source argues that quantum machine learning is promising but not automatically transformative; the decisive research tasks are to solve input and output bottlenecks, produce realistic gate and hardware costings, and benchmark against the best classical methods.\",\n        \"summary_short\": \"This review frames quantum machine learning as the attempt to convert quantum algorithmic speedups into practical learning and data-analysis tools. Its central message is balanced: qBLAS, HHL, qPCA, quantum support vector machines, and quantum Boltzmann machines create plausible advantages, but input, output, costing, and benchmarking problems determine whether those advantages survive in complete systems.\"\n      },\n      \"source_extraction\": {\n        \"converter_name\": \"pdftotext\",\n        \"converter_version\": null,\n        \"quality_status\": \"usable\",\n        \"status\": \"success\",\n        \"warnings\": []\n      },\n      \"source_id\": \"source-05-quantum-machine-learning--a91f0184eb29\",\n      \"source_kind\": \"file\",\n      \"source_locator\": \"05-quantum-machine-learning.pdf\",\n      \"source_media_type\": \"application/pdf\",\n      \"source_semantic\": {\n        \"authors\": [\n          \"Jacob Biamonte\",\n          \"Peter Wittek\",\n          \"Nicola Pancotti\",\n          \"Patrick Rebentrost\",\n          \"Nathan Wiebe\",\n          \"Seth Lloyd\"\n        ],\n        \"display_title\": \"Quantum Machine Learning Speedups and Bottlenecks\"\n      },\n      \"source_type\": \"file\",\n      \"summary\": \"The source reviews quantum machine learning as a field built on possible quantum speedups for linear algebra, optimization, sampling, deep learning, and quantum-data analysis, while emphasizing that input, output, circuit-cost, and benchmarking constraints determine whether these speedups become practical.\",\n      \"title\": \"05-quantum-machine-learning\",\n      \"warnings\": [\n        {\n          \"code\": \"missing_publication_date\",\n          \"message\": \"Publication date could not be resolved in task context; date remains null despite a May 14, 2018 manuscript date in the extracted markdown.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"code\": \"markdown_extraction_usable_not_verified_against_pdf\",\n          \"message\": \"source_extraction.json reports usable pdftotext markdown with no warnings, so the PDF binary was not consulted for layout-sensitive recovery.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"code\": \"claim_refs_are_generated_indices\",\n          \"message\": \"No prior canonical claim IDs were available in the provided context, so evidence and relation endpoints use generated claim indices.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"topics\": [\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-current-two-qubit-gate-and-measurement-error-rates-imply-that-uncorrected-nisq-c--68f47af68b82\",\n        \"claim-lower-gate-error-rates-matter-both-because-they-allow-larger-uncorrected-nisq-ci--043e58605f51\",\n        \"claim-linear-cross-entropy-benchmarking-provides-a-fidelity-measure-by-comparing-exper--4abac38b173d\",\n        \"claim-the-largest-elided-53-qubit-20-cycle-circuits-yielded-f-xeb-2-24-0-21-10-3-over--0a24f12d0686\",\n        \"claim-measured-component-error-rates-under-simultaneous-operation-were-low-enough-to-s--6d6ae35745de\",\n        \"claim-the-largest-random-circuits-combined-53-qubits-1-113-single-qubit-gates-430-two--c8ece98aae87\",\n        \"claim-agreement-among-full-patch-elided-and-predicted-fidelities-on-classically-verifi--c34d4d1d9279\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Preskill's NISQ framing emphasizes that two-qubit gate and measurement errors limit how large uncorrected quantum circuits can grow before noise overwhelms the signal. The Sycamore paper turns that limitation into a measurement problem by estimating fidelity for random circuits whose ideal outputs become difficult to compute directly.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Noise as the benchmark target\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Linear cross-entropy benchmarking compares experimentally observed bitstrings with ideal probabilities computed by classical simulation. Sycamore's component error rates under simultaneous operation supported a model that predicted measurable fidelity for full random circuits, including the largest circuits with 53 qubits, 1,113 single-qubit gates, 430 two-qubit gates, and one measurement per qubit.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Cross-entropy and error models\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The largest elided 53-qubit, 20-cycle circuits yielded F_XEB = (2.24 \\u00b1 0.21) \\u00d7 10^-3 over 30 million samples, supporting average fidelity above 0.1% with 5\\u03c3 confidence. Agreement among full, patch, elided, and predicted fidelities on classically verifiable circuits supports using elided circuits to estimate fidelity when direct full-circuit verification becomes infeasible.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Evidence across verifiable regimes\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb\",\n        \"source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Benchmarking Noisy Quantum Computation\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-benchmarking-noisy-quantum-computation\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-lower-gate-error-rates-matter-both-because-they-allow-larger-uncorrected-nisq-ci--043e58605f51\",\n        \"claim-measured-component-error-rates-under-simultaneous-operation-were-low-enough-to-s--6d6ae35745de\",\n        \"claim-the-largest-random-circuits-combined-53-qubits-1-113-single-qubit-gates-430-two--c8ece98aae87\",\n        \"claim-large-quantum-networks-require-reversible-interconnects-that-convert-quantum-sta--03b0b3a11218\",\n        \"claim-strong-cavity-qed-coupling-supplies-a-physical-regime-in-which-individual-atoms--9807d4e35625\",\n        \"claim-dark-state-adiabatic-control-can-reversibly-map-a-single-photon-optical-field-in--55c6ef7fe1ba\",\n        \"claim-controlled-cavity-pulses-can-generate-entanglement-first-between-an-atom-and-an--ee045e1c7175\",\n        \"claim-electromagnetically-induced-transparency-enables-deterministic-reversible-mappin--b76e4e96d4f0\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"This topic synthesizes the hardware-facing requirement shared by quantum computing and quantum networking sources: useful scaling depends not only on more qubits, but on preserving coherence while operations, measurements, and interfaces become larger and more complex. In the NISQ and Sycamore sources this pressure appears as two-qubit gate errors, readout errors, circuit depth, and predicted fidelity. In the quantum-internet source it appears as the need for reversible interconnects that convert quantum states between matter and light while preserving coherence and entanglement.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Shared concept\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The NISQ source argues that lower gate error rates matter because they permit larger uncorrected circuits and reduce the future overhead of quantum error correction. The Sycamore source gives a concrete processor-scale example: simultaneous-operation component errors were low enough to support a fidelity model, and the largest random circuits combined 53 qubits, 1,113 single-qubit gates, 430 two-qubit gates, and measurement on each qubit with predicted measurable fidelity. These claims make local processor scaling an error-budget problem as much as a qubit-count problem.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Processor scaling\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The quantum-internet source describes the networking analogue through reversible light-matter interfaces, strong cavity-QED coupling, dark-state mappings, controlled cavity pulses, and EIT-based ensemble memories. These mechanisms aim to move quantum states between stationary atoms or ensembles and flying photons without destroying entanglement. A page on this topic can compare local processor scaling with network scaling: both need error-aware physical mechanisms, but networked systems add conversion between matter and light, heterogeneous nodes, and entanglement-preserving interfaces as first-class design constraints.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Network interfaces\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb\",\n        \"source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8\",\n        \"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Coherent interfaces for scaling quantum systems\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-coherent-interfaces-for-scaling-quantum-systems\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-large-scale-quantum-random-access-memory-and-quantum-state-readout-constraints-c--fa6ae1d801a4\",\n        \"claim-the-hhl-linear-system-algorithm-can-output-a-quantum-state-proportional-to-a-sol--9531ddbd1acc\",\n        \"claim-circuit-depth-and-implementation-overhead-can-become-prohibitive-for-nominally-e--07b698d82789\",\n        \"claim-current-two-qubit-gate-and-measurement-error-rates-imply-that-uncorrected-nisq-c--68f47af68b82\",\n        \"claim-scalable-fault-tolerant-quantum-computing-depends-on-quantum-error-correction-bu--cf48bc246343\",\n        \"claim-measured-component-error-rates-under-simultaneous-operation-were-low-enough-to-s--6d6ae35745de\",\n        \"claim-experimental-energy-estimates-are-limited-by-photon-source-impurity-photon-disti--780254eb8ba5\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The QML source states that loading large classical datasets can require exponential time without qRAM and that reading full solution vectors or principal components can be exponentially hard. These caveats constrain linear-algebra speedups such as HHL and qPCA, especially when the desired answer is classical data rather than a quantum state or compact statistic.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Input and output bottlenecks\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"NISQ evidence adds that uncorrected noisy circuits cannot grow far before noise overwhelms the signal, while scalable fault-tolerant computing requires large physical-qubit overhead. Sycamore and VQE sources show useful experiments under these constraints, but also expose fidelity, calibration, and measurement limits.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Hardware noise and fault-tolerance costs\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Across sources, scalability is not determined by asymptotic gate counts alone. Data loading, measurement demands, circuit depth, error rates, and correction overhead jointly decide whether a proposed quantum advantage remains meaningful.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Synthesis claim\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-05-quantum-machine-learning--a91f0184eb29\",\n        \"source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb\",\n        \"source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8\",\n        \"source-04-a-variational-eigenvalue-solver-on-a-photonic-quantum-processor--4678297edf5b\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Data access, readout, and error overheads shape whether quantum algorithms scale\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-data-access-readout-and-error-overheads\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-highly-entangled-quantum-dynamics-is-the-most-natural-arena-for-meaningful-quant--3b6f2f5ba268\",\n        \"claim-nisq-devices-are-best-understood-as-experimental-tools-for-exploring-many-body-q--fde7f9646dbb\",\n        \"claim-random-quantum-circuits-become-exponentially-harder-to-simulate-classically-as-q--f7cd81f80f31\",\n        \"claim-the-sycamore-superconducting-processor-created-random-circuit-quantum-states-on--2b4eeabd4063\",\n        \"claim-the-dlcz-protocol-creates-heralded-entanglement-between-atomic-ensembles-by-inte--aa516185d3b2\",\n        \"claim-atomic-ensemble-experiments-had-achieved-stored-heralded-entanglement-high-retri--7d38956d9ede\",\n        \"claim-scalable-quantum-networks-are-likely-to-be-heterogeneous-systems-that-combine-ca--dfe53fef0772\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"This topic treats entanglement as the common resource behind quantum advantage claims, many-body NISQ exploration, random-circuit hardness, and quantum-network protocols, while also noting that entanglement makes control, verification, and scaling difficult. The NISQ source identifies highly entangled quantum dynamics as a natural arena for meaningful quantum advantage and describes noisy devices as experimental tools for exploring many-body quantum physics. The Sycamore source uses random circuits to create hard-to-simulate 53-qubit quantum states. The quantum-internet source shifts the same resource from one processor to distributed nodes and channels.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Shared concept\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"In the computing sources, entanglement is tied to both opportunity and constraint. Preskill's claims support the idea that many-body entangled dynamics may yield valuable insight before full fault tolerance. Sycamore's random-circuit experiment supports the idea that unstructured output distributions become exponentially harder to simulate as qubits and cycles grow, and that the processor created states in a 2^53-dimensional state space. These claims justify a concept page about entanglement, but not a blanket claim that every entangled device is practically useful.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Computing evidence\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The quantum-internet claims show entanglement as a distributed resource created, stored, retrieved, and verified across physical systems. The DLCZ protocol creates heralded entanglement between atomic ensembles by interfering emitted single-photon fields and conditioning on a detector event, while experiments had achieved stored heralded entanglement and high retrieval efficiencies. Scalable networks are expected to be heterogeneous systems that require stronger entanglement-verification procedures. The synthesis should therefore frame entanglement as necessary for hard quantum behavior and network connectivity, while emphasizing that practical value depends on fidelity, controllability, memory retrieval, and verification.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Network evidence\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb\",\n        \"source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8\",\n        \"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Entanglement as both resource and bottleneck\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-entanglement-as-resource-and-bottleneck\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-device-ansatz-strategies-can-provide-variationally-optimal-solutions-for-the-ava--64cad93bb7ab\",\n        \"claim-unitary-coupled-cluster-ansatz-states-can-be-represented-with-polynomially-many--7f92a3b77387\",\n        \"claim-the-demonstrated-integrated-photonic-processor-prepares-and-measures-arbitrary-t--f41a0c8f645b\",\n        \"claim-large-quantum-networks-require-reversible-interconnects-that-convert-quantum-sta--03b0b3a11218\",\n        \"claim-strong-cavity-qed-coupling-supplies-a-physical-regime-in-which-individual-atoms--9807d4e35625\",\n        \"claim-dark-state-adiabatic-control-can-reversibly-map-a-single-photon-optical-field-in--55c6ef7fe1ba\",\n        \"claim-electromagnetically-induced-transparency-enables-deterministic-reversible-mappin--b76e4e96d4f0\",\n        \"claim-controlled-cavity-pulses-can-generate-entanglement-first-between-an-atom-and-an--ee045e1c7175\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Several sources point toward designing quantum protocols around what a physical platform can reliably prepare, transform, and measure. The photonic variational solver proposes a device ansatz: build the device, characterize the states it can prepare within coherence limits, and optimize within that accessible family. The quantum-internet source treats interfaces similarly at the networking layer, emphasizing reversible conversion between matter and light, cavity-QED interactions, and memory mappings that preserve coherence and entanglement.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Concept\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The shared concept is hardware-native quantum state engineering. Source 04 applies it to computation by using phase shifters, a CNOT gate, photon pairs, and detectors to prepare and measure two-qubit states for energy estimation. Source 03 applies it to communication by using controlled cavity pulses, dark-state adiabatic passage, and electromagnetically induced transparency to map quantum states between stationary matter systems and flying optical fields.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Cross-source synthesis\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"This topic is synthesis-worthy because both computation and networking are constrained by physical interfaces, not just abstract algorithms. The evidence supports a page about platform-shaped protocols, while leaving open which hardware-native approaches will scale best.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Implication\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-04-a-variational-eigenvalue-solver-on-a-photonic-quantum-processor--4678297edf5b\",\n        \"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Hardware-Native Interfaces and Ansatz Design\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-hardware-native-interfaces-and-ansatz-design\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-current-two-qubit-gate-and-measurement-error-rates-imply-that-uncorrected-nisq-c--68f47af68b82\",\n        \"claim-hybrid-qaoa-and-vqe-approaches-are-plausible-nisq-era-testbeds-but-their-ability--31ec67369767\",\n        \"claim-nisq-devices-are-best-understood-as-experimental-tools-for-exploring-many-body-q--fde7f9646dbb\",\n        \"claim-hamiltonian-expectation-values-for-a-broad-class-of-physical-systems-reduce-to-s--ed045d2a43b4\",\n        \"claim-quantum-expectation-estimation-reduces-coherent-evolution-demands-relative-to-ph--e17864953251\",\n        \"claim-the-quantum-variational-eigensolver-prepares-approximate-ground-states-by-using--0037de1d8464\",\n        \"claim-for-the-demonstrated-4x4-hamiltonian-the-quantum-expectation-estimation-approach--8aea71f20a05\",\n        \"claim-experimental-energy-estimates-are-limited-by-photon-source-impurity-photon-disti--780254eb8ba5\",\n        \"claim-nelder-mead-direct-search-was-more-robust-than-gradient-descent-for-the-noisy-ph--c886fbe39dc2\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Near-term quantum algorithms can be framed as hybrid workflows that keep coherent quantum circuits short while outsourcing parameter search and aggregation to classical computation. The photonic variational eigensolver illustrates this pattern by estimating Hamiltonian expectation values through repeated state preparations and Pauli measurements, then using a classical minimizer to improve the Rayleigh-Ritz quotient. Preskill's NISQ discussion supplies the broader constraint: noisy two-qubit gates and measurements limit the useful size of uncorrected circuits, so VQE-like and QAOA-like approaches are plausible testbeds rather than established routes to broad advantage.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Concept\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The shared concept is not simply that hybrid algorithms exist, but that they are architectural responses to limited coherence. Source 04 emphasizes replacing long phase-estimation-style coherent evolution with many short expectation-value measurements; source 01 explains why that trade matters in NISQ hardware, where roughly one thousand fundamental two-qubit operations may already be too many before noise dominates.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Cross-source synthesis\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The evidence supports hybrid shallow algorithms as experimentally meaningful, but not as proven sources of practical speedup. The NISQ source explicitly leaves unresolved whether VQE or QAOA can outperform mature classical approximation methods, while the photonic experiment reports systematic errors, optimizer sensitivity, and degeneracy-related difficulty at larger bond separations.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Limits and open questions\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-04-a-variational-eigenvalue-solver-on-a-photonic-quantum-processor--4678297edf5b\",\n        \"source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Hybrid Shallow Quantum Algorithms Under NISQ Noise\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-hybrid-shallow-quantum-algorithms-under-nisq-noise\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-hybrid-qaoa-and-vqe-approaches-are-plausible-nisq-era-testbeds-but-their-ability--31ec67369767\",\n        \"claim-quantum-computers-are-not-expected-to-efficiently-solve-hard-np-hard-combinatori--0d5f718c4d47\",\n        \"claim-scalable-fault-tolerant-quantum-computing-depends-on-quantum-error-correction-bu--cf48bc246343\",\n        \"claim-noise-resilient-circuit-design-can-extend-nisq-computational-reach-but-it-may-tr--aed4bcfeaa39\",\n        \"claim-the-demonstrated-speedup-is-specific-to-random-circuit-sampling-and-does-not-rem--47d4f349751e\",\n        \"claim-the-largest-elided-53-qubit-20-cycle-circuits-yielded-f-xeb-2-24-0-21-10-3-over--0a24f12d0686\",\n        \"claim-scalable-quantum-networks-are-likely-to-be-heterogeneous-systems-that-combine-ca--dfe53fef0772\",\n        \"claim-atomic-ensemble-experiments-had-achieved-stored-heralded-entanglement-high-retri--7d38956d9ede\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"This topic compares demonstrated or plausible near-term quantum capabilities with the longer path to robust, scalable systems. It is supported across all three sources, but the strength of evidence differs by domain: Sycamore reports a concrete task-specific sampling demonstration, while NISQ algorithms and large quantum networks remain more conditional roadmaps. The topic can help distinguish scientific progress from overclaims about general-purpose quantum computing or fully scalable quantum networking.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Shared concept\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The NISQ source distinguishes experimental value and heuristic testbeds from transformative fault-tolerant applications. QAOA and VQE are plausible NISQ-era testbeds, but their ability to outperform mature classical approximation methods remains unresolved. Quantum computers are not expected to efficiently solve hard NP-hard combinatorial optimization instances, and scalable fault-tolerant quantum computing depends on quantum error correction with heavy physical-qubit and gate overhead. Noise-resilient circuit design may extend reach, but may also make circuits easier to simulate classically.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Near-term computing limits\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The Sycamore source reports random-circuit sampling speedup and an elided 53-qubit, 20-cycle fidelity estimate, while explicitly leaving applications such as Shor-style factoring dependent on fault-tolerant logical qubits. The quantum-internet source surveys primitive but real demonstrations of stored heralded entanglement and retrieval efficiency, yet describes robust scalable network protocols, repeaters, local processing, error-corrected teleportation, and scalable verification as ambitious goals. A page on this concept should state that near-term devices can be scientifically important and sometimes classically hard, while the evidence does not support broad claims that they already deliver general-purpose fault-tolerant quantum systems.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Demonstration versus roadmap\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb\",\n        \"source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8\",\n        \"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Near-term demonstrations versus fault-tolerant quantum systems\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-near-term-demonstrations-versus-fault-tolerant-systems\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-nisq-devices-are-best-understood-as-experimental-tools-for-exploring-many-body-q--fde7f9646dbb\",\n        \"claim-highly-entangled-quantum-dynamics-is-the-most-natural-arena-for-meaningful-quant--3b6f2f5ba268\",\n        \"claim-hybrid-qaoa-and-vqe-approaches-are-plausible-nisq-era-testbeds-but-their-ability--31ec67369767\",\n        \"claim-quantum-computers-are-not-expected-to-efficiently-solve-hard-np-hard-combinatori--0d5f718c4d47\",\n        \"claim-scalable-fault-tolerant-quantum-computing-depends-on-quantum-error-correction-bu--cf48bc246343\",\n        \"claim-the-demonstrated-speedup-is-specific-to-random-circuit-sampling-and-does-not-rem--47d4f349751e\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Preskill characterizes NISQ devices as experimental tools for exploring many-body quantum physics and as stepping stones toward more powerful quantum technologies. This aligns with the Sycamore result as a demonstration of classically hard behavior in a specific sampling task rather than a claim that noisy processors are already broadly useful machines.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Near-term scientific role\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Highly entangled quantum dynamics is presented as the most natural arena for meaningful quantum advantage, and hybrid QAOA or VQE approaches remain plausible NISQ-era testbeds. At the same time, the evidence does not support expecting near-term quantum computers to efficiently solve hard NP-hard optimization instances, and the random-circuit speedup does not by itself establish general application value.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Plausible and uncertain applications\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Both sources separate near-term noisy demonstrations from the longer-term goal of scalable fault-tolerant quantum computing. Preskill emphasizes the heavy physical-qubit overhead of quantum error correction, while the Sycamore source notes that applications such as Shor-style factoring still require fault-tolerant logical qubits.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Fault-tolerant horizon\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb\",\n        \"source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Near-Term Quantum Value Versus Fault-Tolerant Promise\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-near-term-value-versus-fault-tolerant-promise\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-nisq-computers-with-roughly-50-to-a-few-hundred-qubits-mark-a-classically-hard-s--2d5ec194bedf\",\n        \"claim-current-two-qubit-gate-and-measurement-error-rates-imply-that-uncorrected-nisq-c--68f47af68b82\",\n        \"claim-the-sycamore-superconducting-processor-created-random-circuit-quantum-states-on--2b4eeabd4063\",\n        \"claim-for-the-hardest-reported-random-circuit-sampling-task-sycamore-produced-one-mill--07db9778c4a3\",\n        \"claim-random-quantum-circuits-become-exponentially-harder-to-simulate-classically-as-q--f7cd81f80f31\",\n        \"claim-the-demonstrated-speedup-is-specific-to-random-circuit-sampling-and-does-not-rem--47d4f349751e\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Preskill frames devices with roughly 50 to a few hundred noisy qubits as a threshold where brute-force classical simulation becomes difficult, while noise still sharply limits useful computation. The Sycamore experiment provides a concrete example of that threshold: a 53-qubit superconducting processor created random-circuit quantum states in a 2^53-dimensional state space.\",\n          \"heading\": \"NISQ threshold\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The Sycamore result connects the NISQ regime to random-circuit sampling, where output distributions become exponentially harder to simulate classically as qubit count and circuit depth increase. For the hardest reported sampling task, the authors reported one million samples in about 200 seconds and estimated that equal-fidelity classical sampling would require about 10,000 years on one million cores.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Task-specific speedup\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The speedup is evidence for a narrow random-circuit sampling task, not a general solution to near-term quantum computing limitations. Both sources keep the result within a noise-limited NISQ frame and distinguish it from the broader promise of fault-tolerant quantum algorithms.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Limits of the claim\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb\",\n        \"source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"NISQ Scale, Noise, and Random-Circuit Quantum Advantage\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-nisq-scale-and-random-circuit-advantage\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-quantum-machine-learning-can-outperform-classical-algorithms-on-specific-pattern--ade088c638d0\",\n        \"claim-quantum-basic-linear-algebra-subroutines-provide-exponential-speedups-for-fourie--009e83ff60b6\",\n        \"claim-quantum-principal-component-analysis-can-diagonalize-a-covariance-like-density-m--6ee32a171ac2\",\n        \"claim-the-hhl-linear-system-algorithm-can-output-a-quantum-state-proportional-to-a-sol--9531ddbd1acc\",\n        \"claim-the-near-term-viability-of-quantum-machine-learning-depends-on-solving-input-out--0091df09ad90\",\n        \"claim-hybrid-qaoa-and-vqe-approaches-are-plausible-nisq-era-testbeds-but-their-ability--31ec67369767\",\n        \"claim-quantum-computers-are-not-expected-to-efficiently-solve-hard-np-hard-combinatori--0d5f718c4d47\",\n        \"claim-for-the-hardest-reported-random-circuit-sampling-task-sycamore-produced-one-mill--07db9778c4a3\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Quantum machine learning claims identify speedups for pattern recognition, linear algebra, principal components, and linear-system solving, but the same source stresses that input, output, costing, and benchmarking constraints determine whether those speedups become practical. This aligns with NISQ cautions that mature classical approximation methods remain strong competitors and that quantum computers are not expected to efficiently solve hard NP-hard instances in general.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Why asymptotic speedups are not enough\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Random-circuit sampling on Sycamore provides a concrete task-specific super-classical benchmark, while the QML source frames speedup by lower query or gate complexity under particular access assumptions. Together they support a topic centered on distinguishing demonstrable sampling advantage from broadly useful algorithmic advantage.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Task-specific demonstrations versus reusable advantage\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The available evidence supports a cautious synthesis: advantage is credible for selected tasks and models, but each claimed gain depends on hardware errors, data loading, readout, circuit costs, and comparison against optimized classical methods.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Evidence boundaries\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-05-quantum-machine-learning--a91f0184eb29\",\n        \"source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb\",\n        \"source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Practical quantum advantage depends on benchmarks, access models, and classical baselines\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-practical-quantum-advantage-benchmarks-and-bottlenecks\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-nisq-computers-with-roughly-50-to-a-few-hundred-qubits-mark-a-classically-hard-s--2d5ec194bedf\",\n        \"claim-current-two-qubit-gate-and-measurement-error-rates-imply-that-uncorrected-nisq-c--68f47af68b82\",\n        \"claim-quantum-computers-have-credible-grounds-for-super-classical-power-because-of-kno--2f85bd581bbb\",\n        \"claim-random-quantum-circuits-become-exponentially-harder-to-simulate-classically-as-q--f7cd81f80f31\",\n        \"claim-linear-cross-entropy-benchmarking-provides-a-fidelity-measure-by-comparing-exper--4abac38b173d\",\n        \"claim-agreement-among-full-patch-elided-and-predicted-fidelities-on-classically-verifi--c34d4d1d9279\",\n        \"claim-for-the-hardest-reported-random-circuit-sampling-task-sycamore-produced-one-mill--07db9778c4a3\",\n        \"claim-the-demonstrated-speedup-is-specific-to-random-circuit-sampling-and-does-not-rem--47d4f349751e\",\n        \"claim-quantum-connectivity-gives-linked-nodes-a-state-space-that-scales-exponentially--50cc8b700a70\",\n        \"claim-full-density-matrix-characterization-becomes-an-impractical-verification-strateg--65a0be3566e1\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"This topic connects NISQ-era expectations, Sycamore random-circuit sampling, and quantum-network verification around a shared problem: quantum systems can enter classically hard state spaces before they become easy to validate or use fault-tolerantly. Preskill frames roughly 50 to a few hundred noisy qubits as a classically hard threshold while emphasizing that gate errors and measurement errors place serious limits on uncorrected circuits. The Sycamore source reports a task-specific random-circuit sampling demonstration in a 53-qubit state space, while the quantum-internet source shows that quantum connectivity expands the state space across linked nodes and makes direct density-matrix characterization impractical as networks grow.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Shared concept\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The Sycamore claims support quantum advantage through random circuits whose output distributions become exponentially harder to simulate classically, linear cross-entropy benchmarking, and agreement among full, patch, elided, and predicted fidelities on verifiable circuits. That evidence is strong for the reported sampling task, including the estimate that one million samples took about 200 seconds on the processor versus a much larger classical cost at equal fidelity. It remains limited because the demonstrated speedup is specific to random circuit sampling and does not remove the need for fault-tolerant logical qubits for broader applications.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Evidence and limits\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The quantum-network source extends the same verification tension from processors to distributed systems. Quantum connectivity gives linked nodes an exponentially larger joint state space, while full density-matrix characterization becomes an impractical verification strategy. A synthesis page should therefore emphasize both the evidential strength of task-specific benchmarks and the unresolved challenge of scalable certification for noisy processors and heterogeneous quantum networks.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Network extension\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb\",\n        \"source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8\",\n        \"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Quantum advantage under verification and noise limits\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-quantum-advantage-and-verification-limits\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-quantum-computers-have-credible-grounds-for-super-classical-power-because-of-kno--2f85bd581bbb\",\n        \"claim-random-quantum-circuits-become-exponentially-harder-to-simulate-classically-as-q--f7cd81f80f31\",\n        \"claim-linear-cross-entropy-benchmarking-provides-a-fidelity-measure-by-comparing-exper--4abac38b173d\",\n        \"claim-agreement-among-full-patch-elided-and-predicted-fidelities-on-classically-verifi--c34d4d1d9279\",\n        \"claim-for-the-hardest-reported-random-circuit-sampling-task-sycamore-produced-one-mill--07db9778c4a3\",\n        \"claim-the-demonstrated-speedup-is-specific-to-random-circuit-sampling-and-does-not-rem--47d4f349751e\",\n        \"claim-the-photonic-variational-eigensolver-experimentally-recovered-the-he-h-bond-diss--5898494f9b1f\",\n        \"claim-experimental-energy-estimates-are-limited-by-photon-source-impurity-photon-disti--780254eb8ba5\",\n        \"claim-hamiltonian-expectation-values-for-a-broad-class-of-physical-systems-reduce-to-s--ed045d2a43b4\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Claims of quantum advantage depend on matching a hard-to-simulate quantum process with a verification method that remains credible as classical simulation becomes difficult. The Sycamore random-circuit experiment uses linear cross-entropy benchmarking, elided circuits, and component error models to infer fidelity for circuits whose full output distributions are classically expensive. The photonic variational solver uses expectation-value measurements instead of a supremacy benchmark, but it also relies on quantifying experimental errors and comparing measured outputs with theoretical molecular-energy targets.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Concept\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Source 02 and source 04 share a verification problem: useful quantum experiments must report more than raw device output. Sycamore's evidence links observed bitstrings to ideal probabilities and extrapolates fidelity into harder circuits; the photonic solver links detector counts and Pauli-product estimates to molecular energies while identifying systematic shifts from source, circuit, calibration, coupling, and degeneracy effects.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Cross-source synthesis\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The sources support task-specific experimental milestones rather than general-purpose fault-tolerant computation. Sycamore's speedup is tied to random circuit sampling, while the photonic demonstration is a proof-of-principle molecular-energy calculation on a small Hamiltonian. Both require careful caveats about what the measured task does and does not imply.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Boundary of the evidence\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-04-a-variational-eigenvalue-solver-on-a-photonic-quantum-processor--4678297edf5b\",\n        \"source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8\",\n        \"source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Quantum Advantage Requires Verifiable Hardness and Error Models\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-quantum-advantage-requires-verifiable-hardness-and-error-models\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-quantum-machine-learning-for-quantum-data-can-reduce-tomography-and-characteriza--b02ec76133c0\",\n        \"claim-quantum-principal-component-analysis-can-diagonalize-a-covariance-like-density-m--6ee32a171ac2\",\n        \"claim-full-density-matrix-characterization-becomes-an-impractical-verification-strateg--65a0be3566e1\",\n        \"claim-hamiltonian-expectation-values-for-a-broad-class-of-physical-systems-reduce-to-s--ed045d2a43b4\",\n        \"claim-quantum-connectivity-gives-linked-nodes-a-state-space-that-scales-exponentially--50cc8b700a70\",\n        \"claim-scalable-quantum-networks-are-likely-to-be-heterogeneous-systems-that-combine-ca--dfe53fef0772\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Quantum machine learning for quantum data can extract principal components, dynamics, or expectation-value summaries with fewer measurements than full state reconstruction. The quantum-internet source independently warns that full density-matrix characterization fails as networks grow because the state description scales exponentially.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Avoiding full tomography\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The photonic VQE source shows that broad Hamiltonian classes can be reduced to measured Pauli-product expectation values, providing another example of replacing full state knowledge with task-relevant summaries. This connects quantum-data learning, molecular energy estimation, and network verification around the common problem of extracting useful evidence from exponentially large state spaces.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Expectation values as compact observables\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The sources do not establish a universal replacement for tomography. They instead support a family of summary-based strategies whose reliability depends on the target observable, access model, and verification protocol.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Verification uncertainty\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-05-quantum-machine-learning--a91f0184eb29\",\n        \"source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\",\n        \"source-04-a-variational-eigenvalue-solver-on-a-photonic-quantum-processor--4678297edf5b\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Quantum data workflows seek useful summaries without full state reconstruction\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-quantum-data-summaries-without-full-state-reconstruction\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-circuit-depth-and-implementation-overhead-can-become-prohibitive-for-nominally-e--07b698d82789\",\n        \"claim-the-hhl-linear-system-algorithm-can-output-a-quantum-state-proportional-to-a-sol--9531ddbd1acc\",\n        \"claim-the-quantum-variational-eigensolver-prepares-approximate-ground-states-by-using--0037de1d8464\",\n        \"claim-quantum-expectation-estimation-reduces-coherent-evolution-demands-relative-to-ph--e17864953251\",\n        \"claim-device-ansatz-strategies-can-provide-variationally-optimal-solutions-for-the-ava--64cad93bb7ab\",\n        \"claim-current-two-qubit-gate-and-measurement-error-rates-imply-that-uncorrected-nisq-c--68f47af68b82\",\n        \"claim-nisq-devices-are-best-understood-as-experimental-tools-for-exploring-many-body-q--fde7f9646dbb\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The photonic variational eigensolver uses repeated expectation-value measurements inside a classical minimization loop, replacing long coherent phase-estimation evolutions with many short measurements. Quantum machine learning has a parallel theme: quantum subroutines can accelerate selected computations, but circuit depth and implementation overhead can erase nominal exponential gains if realistic costs are not controlled.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Hybrid loops reduce coherent runtime demands\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"NISQ evidence suggests devices with tens to hundreds of noisy qubits may be valuable experimental tools, but gate errors limit uncorrected circuit depth. Hybrid methods such as VQE and QML routines are therefore synthesis-worthy as approaches that seek useful results before fault tolerance, while remaining vulnerable to noise, optimization difficulty, and classical competition.\",\n          \"heading\": \"NISQ fit and limitations\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Device ansatz strategies in the photonic VQE source show one route to adapting algorithms to available hardware. This complements the QML warning that unoptimized realizations, such as large HHL-style circuits, can become prohibitive despite attractive theoretical scaling.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Hardware-aware ansatz design\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-05-quantum-machine-learning--a91f0184eb29\",\n        \"source-04-a-variational-eigenvalue-solver-on-a-photonic-quantum-processor--4678297edf5b\",\n        \"source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Short-coherence hybrid algorithms adapt quantum computation to noisy devices\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-short-coherence-hybrid-algorithms-for-noisy-devices\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_ids\": [\n        \"claim-deep-quantum-learning-can-use-quantum-annealers-programmable-photonic-circuits-a--0bdb973219c3\",\n        \"claim-quantum-boltzmann-machine-training-can-gain-quadratic-improvements-in-thermaliza--2d6a13e72831\",\n        \"claim-the-demonstrated-integrated-photonic-processor-prepares-and-measures-arbitrary-t--f41a0c8f645b\",\n        \"claim-the-photonic-variational-eigensolver-experimentally-recovered-the-he-h-bond-diss--5898494f9b1f\",\n        \"claim-nisq-devices-are-best-understood-as-experimental-tools-for-exploring-many-body-q--fde7f9646dbb\",\n        \"claim-random-quantum-circuits-become-exponentially-harder-to-simulate-classically-as-q--f7cd81f80f31\",\n        \"claim-the-demonstrated-speedup-is-specific-to-random-circuit-sampling-and-does-not-rem--47d4f349751e\"\n      ],\n      \"sections\": [\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The QML source allows deep quantum learning to use quantum annealers, programmable photonic circuits, and quantum Boltzmann machines rather than requiring a large universal quantum computer. This connects to the photonic VQE experiment, which demonstrates a task-specific integrated photonic processor, and to NISQ framing that near-term devices are best understood as experimental tools and stepping stones.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Beyond universal fault-tolerant machines\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"Quantum Boltzmann machine training claims quadratic improvements in thermalization, sampling, and data access under quantum-coherent or qRAM assumptions. Random-circuit sampling and photonic expectation estimation are different tasks, but they share a broader theme: near-term platforms may be evaluated by how well they produce, sample, or measure quantum distributions under constrained coherence.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Sampling and thermalization as shared primitives\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"body\": \"The evidence supports special-purpose platforms as plausible testbeds, not settled replacements for universal fault-tolerant computation. Their value depends on benchmarked performance against classical methods and on whether hardware-specific advantages survive realistic noise and access assumptions.\",\n          \"heading\": \"Open comparison problem\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"source-05-quantum-machine-learning--a91f0184eb29\",\n        \"source-04-a-variational-eigenvalue-solver-on-a-photonic-quantum-processor--4678297edf5b\",\n        \"source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb\",\n        \"source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8\"\n      ],\n      \"structure_type\": \"wiki\",\n      \"title\": \"Special-purpose quantum platforms broaden the near-term learning landscape\",\n      \"topic_id\": \"topic-special-purpose-platforms-for-near-term-quantum-learning\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": []\n}\n--- records ---\n/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/sources/records/source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb.json\n/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/sources/records/source-02-quantum-supremacy-u