{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"set -euo pipefail\nCTX='/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/quantum-physics/outputs/space_overview/subspace--quantum-physics/context.json'\njq -r '\n  .claims\n  | group_by(.source_id)\n  | .[]\n  | \\\"SOURCE \\\" + .[0].source_id + \\\" (\\\" + (length|tostring) + \\\")\\\",\n    (.[] | \\\"  - \\\" + .claim_id + \\\" | \\\" + (.short_title // \\\"\\\") + \\\" | \\\" + .text),\n    \\\"\\\"\n' \\\"\"'$CTX\"'","aggregated_output":"SOURCE source-01-quantum-computing-in-the-nisq-era-and-beyond--2ce59b8ba8bb (10)\n  - claim-current-two-qubit-gate-and-measurement-error-rates-imply-that-uncorrected-nisq-c--68f47af68b82 | Gate errors cap circuits | Current two-qubit gate and measurement error rates imply that uncorrected NISQ circuits cannot grow much beyond about one thousand fundamental two-qubit operations before noise overwhelms the signal.\n  - claim-highly-entangled-quantum-dynamics-is-the-most-natural-arena-for-meaningful-quant--3b6f2f5ba268 | Quantum dynamics is promising | Highly entangled quantum dynamics is the most natural arena for meaningful quantum advantage, and noisy devices around one hundred qubits may already yield valuable physical insight.\n  - claim-hybrid-qaoa-and-vqe-approaches-are-plausible-nisq-era-testbeds-but-their-ability--31ec67369767 | NISQ speedups remain uncertain | Hybrid QAOA and VQE approaches are plausible NISQ-era testbeds, but their ability to outperform mature classical approximation methods remains unresolved.\n  - claim-lower-gate-error-rates-matter-both-because-they-allow-larger-uncorrected-nisq-ci--043e58605f51 | Better gates enable scale | Lower gate error rates matter both because they allow larger uncorrected NISQ circuits and because they reduce the future overhead of fault-tolerant error correction.\n  - claim-nisq-computers-with-roughly-50-to-a-few-hundred-qubits-mark-a-classically-hard-s--2d5ec194bedf | Noise limits NISQ depth | NISQ computers with roughly 50 to a few hundred qubits mark a classically hard simulation regime, but noisy gates place serious near-term limits on what they can compute.\n  - claim-nisq-devices-are-best-understood-as-experimental-tools-for-exploring-many-body-q--fde7f9646dbb | NISQ enables quantum exploration | NISQ devices are best understood as experimental tools for exploring many-body quantum physics and as stepping stones toward more powerful quantum technologies, not as near-term world-changing machines.\n  - claim-noise-resilient-circuit-design-can-extend-nisq-computational-reach-but-it-may-tr--aed4bcfeaa39 | Noise resilience has tradeoffs | Noise-resilient circuit design can extend NISQ computational reach, but it may trade off against classical simulability.\n  - claim-quantum-computers-are-not-expected-to-efficiently-solve-hard-np-hard-combinatori--0d5f718c4d47 | NP-hard problems remain hard | Quantum computers are not expected to efficiently solve hard NP-hard combinatorial optimization instances, although they may provide modest search speedups or useful heuristic approximations.\n  - claim-quantum-computers-have-credible-grounds-for-super-classical-power-because-of-kno--2f85bd581bbb | Quantum advantage has grounds | Quantum computers have credible grounds for super-classical power because of known quantum algorithms, complexity-theoretic sampling arguments, and the absence of efficient classical simulations of general quantum computation.\n  - claim-scalable-fault-tolerant-quantum-computing-depends-on-quantum-error-correction-bu--cf48bc246343 | Error correction remains distant | Scalable fault-tolerant quantum computing depends on quantum error correction, but the physical-qubit overhead makes that era a longer-term objective rather than a near-term NISQ capability.\n\nSOURCE source-02-quantum-supremacy-using-a-programmable-superconducting-processor--f98990eb22d8 (9)\n  - claim-agreement-among-full-patch-elided-and-predicted-fidelities-on-classically-verifi--c34d4d1d9279 | Verification supports extrapolation | Agreement among full, patch, elided, and predicted fidelities on classically verifiable circuits supports using elided circuits to estimate fidelity in the supremacy regime.\n  - claim-for-the-hardest-reported-random-circuit-sampling-task-sycamore-produced-one-mill--07db9778c4a3 | Quantum sampling outpaces classical | For the hardest reported random-circuit sampling task, Sycamore produced one million samples in about 200 seconds while the authors estimated equal-fidelity classical sampling would take about 10,000 years on one million cores.\n  - claim-linear-cross-entropy-benchmarking-provides-a-fidelity-measure-by-comparing-exper--4abac38b173d | XEB measures circuit fidelity | Linear cross-entropy benchmarking provides a fidelity measure by comparing experimentally observed bitstrings with ideal probabilities computed by classical simulation.\n  - claim-measured-component-error-rates-under-simultaneous-operation-were-low-enough-to-s--6d6ae35745de | Gate errors predict fidelity | Measured component error rates under simultaneous operation were low enough to support a model predicting measurable fidelity for full random circuits.\n  - claim-random-quantum-circuits-become-exponentially-harder-to-simulate-classically-as-q--f7cd81f80f31 | Random circuits scale exponentially | Random quantum circuits become exponentially harder to simulate classically as qubit count and circuit depth increase because their output distributions lack exploitable structure.\n  - claim-the-demonstrated-speedup-is-specific-to-random-circuit-sampling-and-does-not-rem--47d4f349751e | Fault tolerance remains necessary | The demonstrated speedup is specific to random circuit sampling and does not remove the need for fault-tolerant logical qubits for applications such as Shor-style factoring.\n  - claim-the-largest-elided-53-qubit-20-cycle-circuits-yielded-f-xeb-2-24-0-21-10-3-over--0a24f12d0686 | Elided circuits retain fidelity | The largest elided 53-qubit, 20-cycle circuits yielded F_XEB = (2.24 ± 0.21) × 10^-3 over 30 million samples, supporting an average fidelity above 0.1% with 5σ confidence.\n  - claim-the-largest-random-circuits-combined-53-qubits-1-113-single-qubit-gates-430-two--c8ece98aae87 | Large circuits remain measurable | The largest random circuits combined 53 qubits, 1,113 single-qubit gates, 430 two-qubit gates, and one measurement per qubit, for which the authors predicted a total fidelity of 0.2%.\n  - claim-the-sycamore-superconducting-processor-created-random-circuit-quantum-states-on--2b4eeabd4063 | Sycamore reaches 53 qubits | The Sycamore superconducting processor created random-circuit quantum states on 53 qubits, corresponding to a computational state space of 2^53, about 10^16 basis states.\n\nSOURCE source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2 (10)\n  - claim-atomic-ensemble-experiments-had-achieved-stored-heralded-entanglement-high-retri--7d38956d9ede | Ensemble experiments store entanglement | Atomic-ensemble experiments had achieved stored heralded entanglement, high retrieval efficiencies, and minimal scalable-node functionality by the time of the source discussion.\n  - claim-controlled-cavity-pulses-can-generate-entanglement-first-between-an-atom-and-an--ee045e1c7175 | Cavity pulses entangle photons | Controlled cavity pulses can generate entanglement first between an atom and an emitted photon and then between sequentially emitted photon polarizations.\n  - claim-dark-state-adiabatic-control-can-reversibly-map-a-single-photon-optical-field-in--55c6ef7fe1ba | Dark states map photons reversibly | Dark-state adiabatic control can reversibly map a single-photon optical field into a trapped atom and later map the stored atomic state back into a flying optical field.\n  - claim-electromagnetically-induced-transparency-enables-deterministic-reversible-mappin--b76e4e96d4f0 | EIT maps photonic memories | Electromagnetically induced transparency enables deterministic reversible mapping of photonic states and photonic entanglement into and out of atomic-ensemble quantum memories.\n  - claim-full-density-matrix-characterization-becomes-an-impractical-verification-strateg--65a0be3566e1 | Verification scales poorly | Full density-matrix characterization becomes an impractical verification strategy as quantum networks grow because the state description scales exponentially with network size.\n  - claim-large-quantum-networks-require-reversible-interconnects-that-convert-quantum-sta--03b0b3a11218 | Networks require quantum interconnects | Large quantum networks require reversible interconnects that convert quantum states between matter and light while preserving coherence and entanglement.\n  - claim-quantum-connectivity-gives-linked-nodes-a-state-space-that-scales-exponentially--50cc8b700a70 | Quantum channels expand state space | Quantum connectivity gives linked nodes a state space that scales exponentially across all nodes, unlike classically connected quantum processors whose state spaces remain separate by node.\n  - claim-scalable-quantum-networks-are-likely-to-be-heterogeneous-systems-that-combine-ca--dfe53fef0772 | Heterogeneous networks need verification | Scalable quantum networks are likely to be heterogeneous systems that combine cavity-QED nodes, ensemble memories, repeaters, local processing, error-corrected teleportation, and stronger entanglement-verification procedures.\n  - claim-strong-cavity-qed-coupling-supplies-a-physical-regime-in-which-individual-atoms--9807d4e35625 | Strong coupling enables interfaces | Strong cavity-QED coupling supplies a physical regime in which individual atoms and photons can interact coherently enough to function as quantum-network nodes and interfaces.\n  - claim-the-dlcz-protocol-creates-heralded-entanglement-between-atomic-ensembles-by-inte--aa516185d3b2 | DLCZ heralds ensemble entanglement | The DLCZ protocol creates heralded entanglement between atomic ensembles by interfering emitted single-photon fields and conditioning on a detector event.\n\nSOURCE source-04-a-variational-eigenvalue-solver-on-a-photonic-quantum-processor--4678297edf5b (10)\n  - claim-device-ansatz-strategies-can-provide-variationally-optimal-solutions-for-the-ava--64cad93bb7ab | Device ansatz optimizes resources | Device ansatz strategies can provide variationally optimal solutions for the available quantum resources even when the ansatz differs from traditional chemistry-motivated forms.\n  - claim-experimental-energy-estimates-are-limited-by-photon-source-impurity-photon-disti--780254eb8ba5 | Noise shifts measured energies | Experimental energy estimates are limited by photon-source impurity, photon distinguishability, circuit imperfections, coupling imbalance, and increasing degeneracy-related difficulty at larger bond separations.\n  - claim-for-the-demonstrated-4x4-hamiltonian-the-quantum-expectation-estimation-approach--8aea71f20a05 | QEE reduces CNOT requirements | For the demonstrated 4x4 Hamiltonian, the quantum expectation-estimation approach uses fewer entangling-gate resources than phase estimation, requiring one CNOT gate instead of at least twelve.\n  - claim-hamiltonian-expectation-values-for-a-broad-class-of-physical-systems-reduce-to-s--ed045d2a43b4 | Hamiltonians decompose into Pauli expectations | Hamiltonian expectation values for a broad class of physical systems reduce to sums of Pauli-product expectation values measured on a quantum state.\n  - claim-nelder-mead-direct-search-was-more-robust-than-gradient-descent-for-the-noisy-ph--c886fbe39dc2 | Nelder Mead handles photon noise | Nelder-Mead direct search was more robust than gradient descent for the noisy photonic optimization landscape and converged to the global minimum in most runs.\n  - claim-quantum-expectation-estimation-reduces-coherent-evolution-demands-relative-to-ph--e17864953251 | QEE reduces coherent evolution | Quantum expectation estimation reduces coherent-evolution demands relative to phase estimation by replacing one long coherent evolution with many short expectation-value measurements.\n  - claim-the-demonstrated-integrated-photonic-processor-prepares-and-measures-arbitrary-t--f41a0c8f645b | Photonic chip prepares two qubits | The demonstrated integrated photonic processor prepares and measures arbitrary two-qubit pure states using voltage-controlled phase shifters, one CNOT gate, photon-pair inputs, and detector count rates.\n  - claim-the-photonic-variational-eigensolver-experimentally-recovered-the-he-h-bond-diss--5898494f9b1f | QVE resolves He-H+ energy | The photonic variational eigensolver experimentally recovered the He-H+ bond-dissociation curve, finding an equilibrium bond length of 92.3±0.1 pm and a ground-state electronic energy of -2.865±0.008 MJ mol^-1 after correction.\n  - claim-the-quantum-variational-eigensolver-prepares-approximate-ground-states-by-using--0037de1d8464 | QVE minimizes Rayleigh Ritz energy | The quantum variational eigensolver prepares approximate ground states by using quantum expectation estimation inside a classical minimization of the Rayleigh-Ritz quotient.\n  - claim-unitary-coupled-cluster-ansatz-states-can-be-represented-with-polynomially-many--7f92a3b77387 | UCC states favor quantum preparation | Unitary coupled-cluster ansatz states can be represented with polynomially many reduced cluster terms and are efficiently preparable on quantum hardware despite lacking a known efficient classical implementation.\n\nSOURCE source-05-quantum-machine-learning--a91f0184eb29 (10)\n  - claim-circuit-depth-and-implementation-overhead-can-become-prohibitive-for-nominally-e--07b698d82789 | Overheads threaten practical speedups | Circuit depth and implementation overhead can become prohibitive for nominally exponential quantum machine learning speedups without substantial algorithmic optimization and realistic cost estimates.\n  - claim-deep-quantum-learning-can-use-quantum-annealers-programmable-photonic-circuits-a--0bdb973219c3 | Special hardware supports deep learning | Deep quantum learning can use quantum annealers, programmable photonic circuits, and quantum Boltzmann machines rather than requiring a large universal quantum computer.\n  - claim-large-scale-quantum-random-access-memory-and-quantum-state-readout-constraints-c--fa6ae1d801a4 | Input output bottlenecks dominate | Large-scale quantum random access memory and quantum-state readout constraints can erase practical advantages for many linear-algebra-based quantum machine learning algorithms on classical data.\n  - claim-quantum-basic-linear-algebra-subroutines-provide-exponential-speedups-for-fourie--009e83ff60b6 | qBLAS enables exponential speedups | Quantum basic linear algebra subroutines provide exponential speedups for Fourier transforms, eigenvalue estimation, eigenvector finding, and linear equation solving relative to the best known classical counterparts.\n  - claim-quantum-boltzmann-machine-training-can-gain-quadratic-improvements-in-thermaliza--2d6a13e72831 | Quantum Boltzmann training accelerates | Quantum Boltzmann machine training can gain quadratic improvements in thermalization, sampling, and training-data access under quantum-coherent or qRAM access assumptions.\n  - claim-quantum-machine-learning-can-outperform-classical-algorithms-on-specific-pattern--ade088c638d0 | Quantum learning can accelerate analysis | Quantum machine learning can outperform classical algorithms on specific pattern-recognition and data-analysis tasks when quantum algorithms exploit coherence, entanglement, or quantum access models.\n  - claim-quantum-machine-learning-for-quantum-data-can-reduce-tomography-and-characteriza--b02ec76133c0 | Quantum data avoids full tomography | Quantum machine learning for quantum data can reduce tomography and characterization costs by extracting principal components, dynamics, or expectation-value summaries without full state reconstruction.\n  - claim-quantum-principal-component-analysis-can-diagonalize-a-covariance-like-density-m--6ee32a171ac2 | qPCA diagonalizes quantum covariance | Quantum principal component analysis can diagonalize a covariance-like density matrix and estimate principal components exponentially more efficiently than classical PCA when data are available in quantum form.\n  - claim-the-hhl-linear-system-algorithm-can-output-a-quantum-state-proportional-to-a-sol--9531ddbd1acc | HHL has conditional advantage | The HHL linear-system algorithm can output a quantum state proportional to a solution vector in polylogarithmic time under sparsity, conditioning, input-preparation, and readout assumptions.\n  - claim-the-near-term-viability-of-quantum-machine-learning-depends-on-solving-input-out--0091df09ad90 | Viability needs practical benchmarks | The near-term viability of quantum machine learning depends on solving input, output, costing, and benchmarking problems rather than only proving asymptotic speedups.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
