rors predict fidelity | Measured component error rates under simultaneous operation were low enough to support a model predicting measurable fidelity for full random circuits.\n  - claim-random-quantum-circuits-become-exponentially-harder-to-simulate-classically-as-q--f7cd81f80f31 | Random circuits scale exponentially | Random quantum circuits become exponentially harder to simulate classically as qubit count and circuit depth increase because their output distributions lack exploitable structure.\n  - claim-the-demonstrated-speedup-is-specific-to-random-circuit-sampling-and-does-not-rem--47d4f349751e | Fault tolerance remains necessary | The demonstrated speedup is specific to random circuit sampling and does not remove the need for fault-tolerant logical qubits for applications such as Shor-style factoring.\n  - claim-the-largest-elided-53-qubit-20-cycle-circuits-yielded-f-xeb-2-24-0-21-10-3-over--0a24f12d0686 | Elided circuits retain fidelity | The largest elided 53-qubit, 20-cycle circuits yielded F_XEB = (2.24 ± 0.21) × 10^-3 over 30 million samples, supporting an average fidelity above 0.1% with 5σ confidence.\n  - claim-the-largest-random-circuits-combined-53-qubits-1-113-single-qubit-gates-430-two--c8ece98aae87 | Large circuits remain measurable | The largest random circuits combined 53 qubits, 1,113 single-qubit gates, 430 two-qubit gates, and one measurement per qubit, for which the authors predicted a total fidelity of 0.2%.\n  - claim-the-sycamore-superconducting-processor-created-random-circuit-quantum-states-on--2b4eeabd4063 | Sycamore reaches 53 qubits | The Sycamore superconducting processor created random-circuit quantum states on 53 qubits, corresponding to a computational state space of 2^53, about 10^16 basis states.\n\nSOURCE source-03-the-quantum-internet--cd7f6b2f11e2\n  - claim-atomic-ensemble-experiments-had-achieved-stored-heralded-entanglement-high-retri--7d38956d9ede | Ensemble experiments store entanglement | Atomic-ensemble experiments had achieved stored heralded entanglement, high retrieval efficiencies, and minimal scalable-node functionality by the time of the source discussion.\n  - claim-controlled-cavity-pulses-can-generate-entanglement-first-between-an-atom-and-an--ee045e1c7175 | Cavity pulses entangle photons | Controlled cavity pulses can generate entanglement first between an atom and an emitted photon and then between sequentially emitted photon polarizations.\n  - claim-dark-state-adiabatic-control-can-reversibly-map-a-single-photon-optical-field-in--55c6ef7fe1ba | Dark states map photons reversibly | Dark-state adiabatic control can reversibly map a single-photon optical field into a trapped atom and later map the stored atomic state back into a flying optical field.\n  - claim-electromagnetically-induced-transparency-enables-deterministic-reversible-mappin--b76e4e96d4f0 | EIT maps photonic memories | Electromagnetically induced transparency enables deterministic reversible mapping of photonic states and photonic entanglement into and out of atomic-ensemble quantum memories.\n  - claim-full-density-matrix-characterization-becomes-an-impractical-verification-strateg--65a0be3566e1 | Verification scales poorly | Full density-matrix characterization becomes an impractical verification strategy as quantum networks grow because the state description scales exponentially with network size.\n  - claim-large-quantum-networks-require-reversible-interconnects-that-convert-quantum-sta--03b0b3a11218 | Networks require quantum interconnects | Large quantum networks require reversible interconnects that convert quantum states between matter and light while preserving coherence and entanglement.\n  - claim-quantum-connectivity-gives-linked-nodes-a-state-space-that-scales-exponentially--50cc8b700a70 | Quantum channels expand state space | Quantum connectivity gives linked nodes a state space that scales exponentially across all nodes, unlike classically connected quantum processors whose state spaces remain separate by node.\n  - claim-scalable-quantum-networks-are-likely-to-be-heterogeneous-systems-that-combine-ca--dfe53fef0772 | Heterogeneous networks need verification | Scalable quantum networks are likely to be heterogeneous systems that combine cavity-QED nodes, ensemble memories, repeaters, local processing, error-corrected teleportation, and stronger entanglement-verification procedures.\n  - claim-strong-cavity-qed-coupling-supplies-a-physical-regime-in-which-individual-atoms--9807d4e35625 | Strong coupling enables interfaces | Strong cavity-QED coupling supplies a physical regime in which individual atoms and photons can interact coherently enough to function as quantum-network nodes and interfaces.\n  - claim-the-dlcz-protocol-creates-heralded-entanglement-between-atomic-ensembles-by-inte--aa516185d3b2 | DLCZ heralds ensemble entanglement | The DLCZ protocol creates heralded entanglement between atomic ensembles by interfering emitted single-photon fields and conditioning on a detector event.\n\nSOURCE source-04-a-variational-eigenvalue-solver-on-a-photonic-quantum-processor--4678297edf5b\n  - claim-device-ansatz-strategies-can-provide-variationally-optimal-solutions-for-the-ava--64cad93bb7ab | Device ansatz optimizes resources | Device ansatz strategies can provide variationally optimal solutions for the available quantum resources even when the ansatz differs from traditional chemistry-motivated forms.\n  - claim-experimental-energy-estimates-are-limited-by-photon-source-impurity-photon-disti--780254eb8ba5 | Noise shifts measured energies | Experimental energy estimates are limited by photon-source impurity, photon distinguishability, circuit imperfections, coupling imbalance, and increasing degeneracy-related difficulty at larger bond separations.\n  - claim-for-the-demonstrated-4x4-hamiltonian-the-quantum-expectation-estimation-approach--8aea71f20a05 | QEE reduces CNOT requirements | For the demonstrated 4x4 Hamiltonian, the quantum expectation-estimation approach uses fewer entangling-gate resources than phase estimation, requiring one CNOT gate instead of at least twelve.\n  - claim-hamiltonian-expectation-values-for-a-broad-class-of-physical-systems-reduce-to-s--ed045d2a43b4 | Hamiltonians decompose into Pauli expectations | Hamiltonian expectation values for a broad class of physical systems reduce to sums of Pauli-product expectation values measured on a quantum state.\n  - claim-nelder-mead-direct-search-was-more-robust-than-gradient-descent-for-the-noisy-ph--c886fbe39dc2 | Nelder Mead handles photon noise | Nelder-Mead direct search was more robust than gradient descent for the noisy photonic optimization landscape and converged to the global minimum in most runs.\n  - claim-quantum-expectation-estimation-reduces-coherent-evolution-demands-relative-to-ph--e17864953251 | QEE reduces coherent evolution | Quantum expectation estimation reduces coherent-evolution demands relative to phase estimation by replacing one long coherent evolution with many short expectation-value measurements.\n  - claim-the-demonstrated-integrated-photonic-processor-prepares-and-measures-arbitrary-t--f41a0c8f645b | Photonic chip prepares two qubits | The demonstrated integrated photonic processor prepares and measures arbitrary two-qubit pure states using voltage-controlled phase shifters, one CNOT gate, photon-pair inputs, and detector count rates.\n  - claim-the-photonic-variational-eigensolver-experimentally-recovered-the-he-h-bond-diss--5898494f9b1f | QVE resolves He-H+ energy | The photonic variational eigensolver experimentally recovered the He-H+ bond-dissociation curve, finding an equilibrium bond length of 92.3±0.1 pm and a ground-state electronic energy of -2.865±0.008 MJ mol^-1 after correction.\n  - claim-the-quantum-variational-eigensolver-prepares-approximate-ground-states-by-using--0037de1d8464 | QVE minimizes Rayleigh Ritz energy | The quantum variational eigensolver prepares approximate ground states by using quantum expectation estimation inside a classical minimization of the Rayleigh-Ritz quotient.\n  - claim-unitary-coupled-cluster-ansatz-states-can-be-represented-with-polynomially-many--7f92a3b77387 | UCC states favor quantum preparation | Unitary coupled-cluster ansatz states can be represented with polynomially many reduced cluster terms and are efficiently preparable on quantum hardware despite lacking a known efficient classical implementation.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
